          "content": "Honestly, the 8 checks are solid for the core cases. But there are 3 real gaps worth filling:\n\n---\n\n**Gap 1 — Headline text actually rendered on a bright patch**\n\nRight now `headline_contrast` measures the mean brightness of the entire headline zone. But the zone could be mostly dark with one bright patch (sky, white wall) right behind the headline text. The mean looks fine but the text is actually unreadable on that patch.\n\nA better check: sample the 5 pixels directly behind each rendered text line, not the zone average. This is harder to do post-compose, but a reasonable proxy is measuring the **standard deviation of brightness in the top-left 40% of the card** — high std means a bright patch exists under the dark overlay and could bleed through.\n\n**Worth adding? Yes.** Takes 4 lines.\n\n---\n\n**Gap 2 — Image is too blurry to feel credible**\n\nA blurry or heavily pixelated photo looks amateurish. News pages that post blurry images lose trust fast. You can measure this with a Laplacian variance — the standard blur detection technique. Sharp image = high variance. Blurry = low variance.\n\n```\nblur_score = variance of Laplacian of center crop\nif blur_score < threshold → reject\n```\n\nThis catches Pollinations.ai generated images that come back soft or noisy, and Pexels thumbnails accidentally used at wrong resolution.\n\n**Worth adding? Yes.** High-value, 6 lines.\n\n---\n\n**Gap 3 — Badge is readable (not lost against photo)**\n\nThe badge sits top-left at `(ML, MT)` = `(72, 60)`. If the photo there is very bright (blue sky, white building), the navy badge blends in — which is why we added the drop shadow. But the shadow might not be enough on a very light background.\n\nCheck: mean brightness of the 10px zone immediately surrounding the badge. If it's above 180, the badge needs a stronger shadow or a semi-transparent scrim behind it.\n\n**Worth adding? Maybe.** Lower priority since the drop shadow already handles most cases.\n\n---\n\n**What I'd skip**\n\n- Checking if the \"main subject is visible\" more precisely (face detection, saliency maps) — requires OpenCV or a model, too heavy for a publishing pipeline\n- Checking text color explicitly — it's always WHITE, baked into the design, no need\n- Checking aspect ratio — it's always 1200×1500, fixed in code\n\n---\n\n**My recommendation: add Gap 1 and Gap 2 only.** Gap 3 is already handled by the shadow. Want me to add them?"
