    "Here are the improvements I'd make, ranked by impact:\n\n---\n\n**1. Saturation detection — \"everyone already covered this\"**\n\nIf 4+ sources all published the exact same story in the last 6 hours, it's already saturated. Publishing it now adds nothing new to your audience's feed. The gate should detect this and reject unless your post angle is genuinely different.\n\n---\n\n**2. Contradiction detection**\n\nIf two stories in the current batch make opposing factual claims about the same subject (\"North Korea fires missile\" vs \"North Korea denies missile test\"), neither should publish until one is confirmed. Right now both could pass independently.\n\n---\n\n**3. Source-claim credibility scoring**\n\n\"Anonymous sources say...\" is weaker than \"The Pentagon confirmed...\". The gate should detect attribution strength in the summary text and downgrade stories built entirely on anonymous or unverified claims — not reject, but hold pending corroboration.\n\n---\n\n**4. Headline-to-body consistency check**\n\nThe headline says \"100 killed\" but the summary only mentions \"casualties reported.\" That's a misleading headline even without a clickbait pattern. A basic numeric/claim extraction check comparing title claims against summary facts would catch this.\n\n---\n\n**5. Time-of-day relevance gate**\n\nSports scores and match results posted at 3 AM local time have near-zero engagement. The gate should hold time-sensitive content (scores, live events, award results) if it's outside the active audience window, and release it automatically when the window opens.\n\n---\n\n**6. Story velocity check**\n\nA story published 10 minutes ago with no follow-up from any other source is unverified breaking news — high risk. The gate should flag stories that are *too fresh* with *no corroboration* as HOLD, not PUBLISH. Right now freshness is rewarded unconditionally.\n\n---\n\n**7. Duplicate image detection**\n\nTwo different stories might both pull the same Pexels stock photo (e.g. \"generic courtroom\" image used for every crime story). Your page would look repetitive. The gate should track the last 20 image IDs used and reject reuse.\n\n---\n\n**8. Geo-relevance signal for your audience**\n\nIf your audience is primarily English-speaking and global, a story entirely about local municipal politics in a country with no international significance should be held. The gate can check for proper nouns and geographic signals against a known \"global significance\" word list.\n\n---\n\nThe most valuable to build first: **#4 (headline-body consistency)**, **#6 (story velocity/too-fresh check)**, and **#3 (source attribution strength)**. These three close the biggest gaps that the current 11 checks miss — stories that *look* legitimate but are built on shaky foundations.\n\nWant me to implement them?\n[Tool uses: none]",
