    "Here are the ones worth adding, ranked by impact:\n\n---\n\n**1. Scheduled window awareness for the SCHEDULE lane**\n\nRight now SCHEDULE just enforces a 1-hour gap. But you have three defined windows: 07:30, 12:30, 19:30. A SCHEDULE-lane story should wait for the *next* window, not just any point 60 minutes after the last post. A tech announcement at 10:45 AM should hold until 12:30, not publish at 11:45 just because an hour passed.\n\nThis makes the page feel curated rather than random.\n\n---\n\n**2. Story expiry per lane**\n\nA HOLD story queued at 09:00 AM that never surfaced should expire by end of day — not linger and potentially publish at 11 PM when the news cycle has moved on. Different lanes should have different TTLs:\n\n- PUBLISH_NOW: 2 hours (breaking news is stale fast)\n- NEXT_SLOT: 6 hours\n- SCHEDULE: 12 hours\n- HOLD: 18 hours (current day only)\n\nCurrently you rely on the 48-hour global purge, which is far too generous for most lanes.\n\n---\n\n**3. Re-routing on expiry**\n\nWhen a PUBLISH_NOW story sits unposted for 2 hours (e.g., agent was offline), it shouldn't silently expire — it should be *downgraded* to NEXT_SLOT with a note. The event is no longer breaking, but the story still has value. Same for NEXT_SLOT → SCHEDULE after 6 hours.\n\n---\n\n**4. A simple audit log**\n\nRight now you can see what published, but you can't easily answer \"why did this story get rejected?\" or \"what was held today?\" A lightweight `posting_decisions.jsonl` — one line per story, recording title, score, lane, decision, reason, and timestamp — would let you tune the thresholds over time with real data instead of guessing.\n\nWithout this, you're flying blind when calibrating scores.\n\n---\n\n**5. Token-bucket rate limit for PUBLISH_NOW**\n\nRight now multiple PUBLISH_NOW stories can publish back-to-back with zero gap. If three earthquakes happen in an hour, the page floods. PUBLISH_NOW should still bypass the routine interval, but a minimum 10-minute gap between any two PUBLISH_NOW posts prevents the page from looking like a panic feed.\n\n---\n\n**6. Slot reservation**\n\nIf a SCHEDULE story is queued for the 19:30 window, and 4 other SCHEDULE stories are also waiting, only the highest-scoring one should take the slot — the rest re-evaluate at the next day's windows. Currently all SCHEDULE stories compete to publish the moment the interval clears, which can mean 4 posts in 10 minutes at 19:30.\n\n---\n\nThe three I'd build first: **#1 (window awareness)**, **#4 (audit log)**, and **#2 (per-lane TTL)**. Together they close the biggest gaps: timing precision, observability, and stale-story prevention.\n\nWant me to implement them?\n[Tool uses: none]",
