    "Here's what I see as the remaining gaps, ranked by impact:\n\n---\n\n**1. The audit log has no reader**\n\n`posting_decisions.jsonl` is being written but there's no way to query it. You can't answer \"what did we reject today and why?\" without manually grepping a raw file. A simple `python audit.py` command that prints a daily summary — rejections by reason, holds by category, publish rate by lane — would turn the log into actual intelligence for tuning thresholds.\n\n---\n\n**2. Window slots are still first-come-first-served across categories**\n\nRight now the 12:30 window takes the top-scoring SCHEDULE story regardless of category. If three politics stories are the top three, the 12:30 window publishes politics, then waits until 19:30 for the next slot. A window should ideally publish a mix: one Tier 1, one Tier 2, one Tier 3 — spread across different categories. Variety within a window makes the page look like a newsroom, not a beat reporter.\n\n---\n\n**3. No feedback loop between publish performance and scoring**\n\nEvery story is scored the same way regardless of how similar past stories performed. If every technology story you've published in the last week has been from the same source, and you're about to publish another one, the system has no memory of that pattern. A light recency bias — slightly boosting categories that haven't been published recently — would naturally diversify the feed without manual tuning.\n\n---\n\n**4. Token expiry is silent until it fails**\n\nRight now a token expiry causes a `FatalPublishError` mid-run. There's no proactive check. Before starting a publishing run, the agent should validate the token with a lightweight Graph API call (`/me?fields=id`) and fail fast with a clear message rather than discovering expiry after generating an image and writing an AI post.\n\n---\n\n**5. No recovery for partial runs**\n\nIf the agent publishes 3 stories and then crashes (network error, token expiry, machine sleep), the 4 remaining queued QUEUED entries are orphaned. They sit in the queue, their TTL ticks down, and the next run re-evaluates them. This actually works fine — but there's no log entry or summary of what happened in the interrupted run. You'd want a `run_log.jsonl` that records each run: start time, how many published, how many failed, what stopped it.\n\n---\n\n**6. Image fallback has no quality gate**\n\nWhen Pexels returns an image, there's no check that the image is actually relevant to the story. A story about an earthquake might get a photo of a beach because \"earth\" matched. The `IMAGE_MISMATCH` DNP check uses keyword overlap on the query string, but that check is against `story.title` vs `image_query` — both of which are derived from the same title, so the check always passes. The actual Pexels query terms should be compared against the image's Pexels tags/description, not the title again.\n\n---\n\n**7. The scheduler has no health check**\n\n`scheduler.py` runs indefinitely with `time.sleep(60)` loops. If the agent process hangs — Ollama is slow, Pexels times out — the scheduler doesn't know and the next window fires while the previous one is still running. Two concurrent publishing runs can cause duplicate posts. The scheduler should track whether the previous run is still active before starting a new one.\n\n---\n\nThe four I'd build first: **#4 (token pre-validation)**, **#7 (scheduler health check)**, **#1 (audit reader)**, and **#2 (window category mix)**. These are the ones that affect reliability and day-to-day usability most directly.\n\nWant me to implement them?\n[Tool uses: none]",
